 "result": "I created the Dockerfile and two supporting files in the repo. I could not build or run the image here, so none of it has been tested.\n\nFiles created:\n- `Dockerfile`\n- `.dockerignore`\n- `docker/healthcheck.js` (used by the Dockerfile's `HEALTHCHECK`)\n\nNo existing files were modified.\n\nHow the image is set up:\n- **Build:** two stages on `node:22-bookworm-slim`, matching the Node 22 LTS that `scripts/security-check.sh` expects. The first stage runs `npm ci --omit=dev`, so Puppeteer's Chromium download is skipped. Build tools are present in that stage only, as a fallback if `better-sqlite3` has to compile.\n- **Runtime:** runs as the `node` user with `NODE_ENV=production`, because `.env.docker` says the production image refuses placeholder or short `JWT_SECRET` values. User data goes to `/data` (declared as a volume, set via `DATA_DIR`). `tini` is PID 1, because the server has no SIGTERM handler.\n- **Copied into the image:** `server/`, `ui/`, `docs/` (the guidance store reads `docs/architecture`), `scripts/`, `package.json`, `LICENSE`, and `data/demo-bundles/`.\n- **`.dockerignore`:** excludes `.env*`, `ssl/`, `node_modules`, `tests/`, `tools/`, and the committed `data/*.json` seed files. This follows `.gitguardian.yaml`, which says `data/` is not baked into the image.\n\nThings you should know before running it:\n- **Secret:** the container exits at startup unless `JWT_SECRET` is at least 32 characters and not the placeholder. Run `cp .env.docker .env`, set the value (`openssl rand -hex 32`), then pass `--env-file .env`.\n- **Demo accounts:** a fresh `/data` gets the built-in demo accounts (`admin`/`adminpass`, and others). Set real passwords with `docker exec -it isms-builder node scripts/set-password.js admin`, or use `scripts/prepare-production.js`.\n- **Bind mounts:** if you mount a host directory over `/data`, it must be writable by uid 1000 (`chown -R 1000:1000 <dir>`).\n- **Compose file:** `.env.docker` and `scripts/backup-and-deploy.sh` both refer to `docker compose`, but no `docker-compose.yml` exists. I did not create one because you asked only for the Dockerfile and its supporting files.\n\nTo check it yourself: `docker build -t isms-builder .`, then run it with `-p 3000:3000 --env-file .env -v isms-data:/data`, and confirm `http://localhost:3000/ui/login.html` loads. The first build is the one most likely to surface a problem, from the `npm ci` step or the `better-sqlite3` install.",
